<h2>A decade-old stamping battle</h2>
<p>In March 2014, Religare extended four loan facilities to Zee group companies under loan agreements executed on 15 March 2014. The stamping of those agreements became a procedural war of its own: a 2020 order under Section 16 holding the Maharashtra Stamp Act applicable, a 2022 judgment of the High Court holding the Indian Stamp Act applicable, and a series of Supreme Court orders in 2022 that shaped a limited remand. The arbitrator&rsquo;s interim award of 28 February 2026 answered the remanded question: the agreements were governed by the Maharashtra Stamp Act, attracting a fixed duty of Rs 100 under Article 5(h)(B), and the duty paid was sufficient. The judgment records that &ldquo;the scope of the remand was limited to the question of the applicable stamp law and the adequacy of stamp duty thereof&rdquo;.</p>
<p>Both camps attacked the interim award from opposite directions — the borrowers contending the correct entry was Article 5(h)(A)(iv)(b) with ad valorem duty of 0.2%, Religare contesting the stamp-law holding from the other side.</p>

<h2>Holding one: an interim award is challengeable now</h2>
<p>The threshold objection was that a Section 34 petition does not lie against an interim award. The court rejected it. An interim award is an &ldquo;arbitral award&rdquo; within Section 2(1)(c); Section 31(6) empowers the tribunal to decide &ldquo;any matter&rdquo; conclusively by interim award; and a conclusive determination of the applicable stamp law fits that description. Importantly, the court noted that such a decision &ldquo;does not, in any way, affect the jurisdiction of the tribunal to continue&rdquo; — the reference proceeds while the interim award faces scrutiny.</p>

<h2>Holding two: stamping is not a Section 16 question</h2>
<p>The companion objection sought to route the matter through Section 16 — treating the stamping determination as a ruling on the tribunal&rsquo;s own jurisdiction. The court read <em>In Re: Interplay</em> narrowly: the stamp-law question in this reference &ldquo;has nothing to do with the Tribunal&rsquo;s own jurisdiction&rdquo;. Not every substantive stamping issue is a jurisdictional objection; here it was a question on the merits of the remanded issue. The conclusion followed: &ldquo;the present batch of cross-objection petitions filed under Section 34 of the Act are maintainable&rdquo;, and the court proceeded to examine the rival stamp-law contentions.</p>
<div class="note">
<p><strong>Scope of this note:</strong> this article addresses the maintainability holdings of the judgment. The court&rsquo;s detailed treatment of the competing entries under the Maharashtra Stamp Act and the Indian Stamp Act proceeds from that threshold and should be read in full from the judgment itself.</p>
</div>

<h2>Why the maintainability ruling matters</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Limitation discipline.</strong> If an interim award is independently challengeable, the Section 34(3) clock — three months plus a condonable thirty days — runs from its receipt. Parties who wait for the final award may find the interim determination beyond challenge.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Strategic bifurcation.</strong> Tribunals increasingly decide threshold issues — stamping, limitation, liability in principle — by interim award. Each such award is a potential satellite challenge, and parties should budget for it.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Section 16 is not a catch-all.</strong> The jurisdictional route has its own remedial consequences. Labelling a merits issue as jurisdictional will not change the applicable challenge mechanism.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Commercial Division practice.</strong> Cross-petitions by both sides against one interim award can be heard together, as here, with common questions framed at the outset.</div>
</div>

<h2>The larger arc</h2>
<p>Stamping objections have shadowed Indian arbitration for a decade, through <em>SMS Tea Estates</em>, <em>Garware</em>, <em>N.N. Global</em> and the seven-judge course correction in <em>In Re: Interplay</em>. This judgment shows where that arc has settled in day-to-day Commercial Division practice: stamping no longer stalls references at the threshold, but it remains live on the merits — and when a tribunal resolves it by interim award, Section 34 is the door through which the losing side may immediately walk.</p>
